// Licensing and publication — what is published, and the public term
// behind each omission.

= 5. Licensing and publication

What may be published decides what the corpus carries, and the repository's LICENSE file states the terms and attribution for each source. Published are the codes, hierarchies, and validity of every system; the mappings with their comments and grades; and the project's own work — schema, tool, registries, the English KVÅ renderings, and the laboratory mapping material. The designation catalogues of the Swedish national classifications are not redistributed: those systems carry codes, hierarchy, and validity. Where a review judged a mapping, its rationale may quote the single title it judged from the Swedish national classifications or TLV's published lists, with the source named, under the quotation right of the Swedish Copyright Act; RETTS triage titles are not quoted.

Each omission rests on a publicly stated term. WHO's ICD-10, 2016 edition, carries an all-rights-reserved imprint and the 2019 edition is offered under CC BY-ND 3.0 IGO; neither licenses redistribution of designation text, and the same license reserves crosswalks between other classifications and ICD-10 to a separate written agreement, the clause under which the ICD-10 crosswalk collections (21,920 mappings) and the ICD-11 layers of chapter 4 are held outside the corpus. The Swedish classifications are protected by copyright with quotation permitted, and the publishers' terms grant no license to redistribute their designation catalogues, so KVÅ, KSI, SPKOD, ICD-10-SE, and the Swedish ICF are carried as codes, with single titles quoted only as review evidence. The Nordic procedure classification has no public reuse license, and the triage point names belong to a commercially licensed product: both code-only. The pharmaceutical registers are published for reuse under lag (2022:818), but without an explicit license text, so they are carried as codes, with single titles quoted only as review evidence. The designations the corpus does carry are carried under their own terms: NPU verbatim under the NPU terms of use with attribution, SCB under CC0, and RxNorm names as U.S. public domain with the NLM acknowledgement. SNOMED CT, LOINC, MedDRA, and UCUM are referenced by code only, under the reader's own license with each owner; EDQM standard terms are referenced by code and OMOP concept identifiers by id alone; Orphanet codes are reused under CC BY 4.0 with the attribution LICENSE gives.

The corpus is versioned by repository release, and every mapping collection names its own version, 2026.1 throughout this release.
